    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Wheeze, night cough, and breathlessness are limiting his training and his long days at work. [S]</item><item>Would rather control his asthma with exercise than with daily medicine. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Theodore Roosevelt was born at 28 East 20th Street, New York City, on October 27, 1858. [D]</item><item>In his autobiography Roosevelt wrote that he was a sickly, delicate boy who suffered much from asthma and frequently had to be taken away on trips to find a place where he could breathe; he remembered sitting up in bed gasping at night as a very small child. [D]</item><item>When he was nearly fourteen, during an attack of asthma, he was sent alone to Moosehead Lake; after two other boys on the stage-coach easily overpowered him, he began boxing lessons and a long program of physical training, with his father&apos;s approval. [D]</item><item>Roosevelt wrote that he was very near-sighted as a boy without knowing it, and that at about thirteen, after he could not read a distant billboard, he got his first pair of spectacles. [D]</item><item>A 2015 review of the historical record in the Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology: In Practice concluded that his respiratory problems began in early childhood, that the record gives strong evidence for poorly controlled persistent asthma, that the asthma became relatively quiet in adolescence when his exercise program began at about 12, and that he continued to have serious exacerbations in adolescence and adulthood; it found little if any support for the claim that his asthma was psychosomatic. [R]</item><item>Roosevelt received his Bachelor of Arts degree from Harvard in June 1880 and was elected to the New York State Assembly in 1881. [D]</item><item>His father, Theodore Roosevelt Sr., died in 1878 shortly after being diagnosed with stomach cancer. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

